hmann scalar $K$ of the three-brane against the isotropic radius $\\rho$ across the sheet, the same at every moment and at every point of the sheet and of the sphere, each point of the plot a 5-sphere of areal radius $(\\rho^4 + L^4)^{1/4}$. The Ricci scalar $R$ vanishes here and in every other chart, as the trace of the field equations asks of a self-dual 5-form in ten dimensions.","Toward $\\rho = 0$ the 1 in $H = 1 + L^4/\\rho^4$ drops out, the spheres close on the radius $L$, and $K$ closes on $80/L^4$: it is already $79.37/L^4$ at $\\rho = L/5$, within a percent of that constant. It falls at every radius, through $32.5/L^4$ at $\\rho = L$, and far out it falls off as $960L^8/\\rho^{12}$ into the flat space the sheet sits in, which it is within 2 percent of by $5.612 \\times 10^{-5}/L^4$ at $\\rho = 4\\,L$. The horizon $\\rho = 0$ lies infinitely far down the throat, at the edge of the axis, where $g^{\\rho\\rho} = \\rho^2/\\sqrt{\\rho^4 + L^4}$ vanishes.","The level across the plot is the throat's own $K = 80/L^4$, the constant its two charts publish at every radius, anti-de Sitter space of five dimensions times a sphere of five, both of radius $L$."],"settings":"$L = 1$, the unit of every length, the radius of the throat and of its sphere, as its other drawings take it.","clip":"We draw $K\\,L^4$ on a logarithmic scale from $10^{-5}$ to $10^2$; every value drawn lies within it, and the curve starts $10^{-3}\\,L$ from $\\rho = 0$, which the chart leaves out."}]}
